{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_b4zym4zuvgblvuwljjgfiod6y5stcx6b3iekm3excm474d2cfjkq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01LemEyQtXKQdcGdhPJk4jfV","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-pTQYiQ/translated_rust/src/main.rs","old_string":"pub fn main() {\n    let mut x: i32 = 0;\n    let stdin = io::stdin();\n    let mut input = stdin.lock();\n    if let Some(v) = scanf_d(&mut input) {\n        x = v;\n    }\n    driver(x);\n}","new_string":"/// Faithful translation of the C `main` body: read one integer, call `driver`,\n/// return `0`.\n///\n/// This is the part that is shared verbatim with the `cdylib`'s exported `main`\n/// (see `src/ffi.rs`); it deliberately touches no process-global state, exactly\n/// like the C `main`.\npub fn run() {\n    let mut x: i32 = 0;\n    let stdin = io::stdin();\n    let mut input = stdin.lock();\n    if let Some(v) = scanf_d(&mut input) {\n        x = v;\n    }\n    driver(x);\n}\n\n/// Restores the default `SIGPIPE` disposition.\n///\n/// Rust's runtime installs `SIG_IGN` for `SIGPIPE` before `main` runs, whereas a\n/// C program inherits the default (`SIG_DFL`) disposition. Without this, writing\n/// the result into a pipe whose read end has been closed makes the C program die\n/// from `SIGPIPE` (exit status 141) while the Rust program would silently ignore\n/// the failed `write` and exit `0`. Undoing Rust's default keeps the executable's\n/// observable behaviour identical to the C executable's.\n///\n/// Only the *executable* entry point does this: the C `main` compiled into a\n/// shared object does not change signal dispositions either, so the `cdylib`\n/// export in `src/ffi.rs` calls [`run`] directly instead.\n#[cfg(unix)]\nfn restore_default_sigpipe() {\n    const SIGPIPE: i32 = 13;\n    const SIG_DFL: usize = 0;\n    extern \"C\" {\n        fn signal(signum: i32, handler: usize) -> usize;\n    }\n    // SAFETY: `signal` with `SIG_DFL` is async-signal-safe and cannot fail for\n    // the valid signal number `SIGPIPE`; the returned previous handler is\n    // discarded.\n    unsafe {\n        signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL);\n    }\n}\n\n#[cfg(not(unix))]\nfn restore_default_sigpipe() {}\n\npub fn main() {\n    restore_default_sigpipe();\n    run();\n}"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1850,"cache_read_input_tokens":49514,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1850,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"48be53e8-e7f1-4c70-a7a7-6c40e01deca5","uuid":"eea6ecf0-67aa-4ebd-af62-181ba1c6b066","timestamp":"2026-08-18T12:40:40.475Z"}
